so what's happened in the jobs market  over the last 18 months is perhaps one  of the greatest curiosities of this  crisis normally at times of recession we  see unemployment spiking we see  vacancies falling right down although  unemployment has been kept relatively in  check vacancies have actually behaved in  entirely the opposite way to what we  might have expected just this morning we  heard vacancies in september up record  highs again over 1.1 million and  although that might seem like good news  on the surface for workers the reality  might be a bit more nuanced and there's  big challenges for business about how to  realign to the new reality post pandemic  so first of all have a look at this what  we're plotting here is some of the the  key sectors that are driving that big  growth in vacancies up there at the top  transport and logistics we've heard so  much about it haven't we up 300 job  adverts there on what they were pre  pandemic but problems in other sectors  too manufacturing construction even  travel and tourism surprising perhaps  given the problems that that sector has  faced  uh however that's not the case uh in all  sectors across the economy have a look  at this we've plotted here is some  sectors where job vacancies are actually  down on what they were before the  pandemic some of this might not be that  surprising for example sales we know  retail has had some problems but some  sectors here are a little bit surprising  jobs down in energy jobs down in legal  graduate jobs down as well and what's  essentially showing us is that there's a  realignment going to be happening in the  economy we know for example don't we  that there can be fewer jobs in places  for example like city centre sandwich  shops more jobs in places like  warehouses it may be a bit too soon to  see some of these things really playing  out in the data but some of the early  signs are certainly interesting now the  question for many of us is not just  where are the jobs but actually where  are the workers to fill all of these  vacancies and it's quite interesting  they might not necessarily always be in  the right places so what we've done here  is charting the number of unemployed and  furloughed people as of july against the  number of  regions of the country so essentially  the higher up they are on this graph the  more unemployed and furloughed people  there were as of july the further along  they are uh the greater growth in  vacancies that those sectors have seen  what this essentially shows there's  different problems in different regions  london for example right up there at the  top absolutely the worst place to be in  the country right now if you're a worker  by far the highest number of people  potentially looking for work the fewest  vacancies over at the other side  different problems the north east at the  largest number of vacancies there  yorkshire and the humber where we are  today higher vacancies and comparatively  fewer people looking for those jobs so  not only might the workers not be in the  right places but they might not also be  at the right skill level have a look at  this there is a slight correlation  between the number of vacancies in  certain sectors and the average earnings  in those sectors so over at this side  hospitality many more vacancies lower  pay on average over there on the other  end finance worth saying anecdotally  transport up there the big vacancies  driver anecdotally we do know that some  of the jobs that do exist are in those  lower paying driving jobs as opposed to  the managerial jobs as well and look as  we go forward over the coming years and  the coming decades there will be other  new challenges for businesses as well i  want to leave you with this one of the  challenges we may all face is automation  what this essentially shows is that  there is a direct correlation uh  anecdotally between  how likely a sector was to furlough its  workers in the recent crisis and how at  risk that sector might be from  automation so look up here at the top  food and tourism retail construction to  a degree as well highly likely to have  furloughed their workers during this  crisis much more at risk of automation  over here on the opposite end media  scientific i.t of course it won't go  unnoticed that these tend to be the  higher paying careers as well  potentially a slightly lower paying over  there so we big questions for businesses  not just about how these factors all  collide are we creating more vulnerable  group of workers going forward but  crucially also how business is going to  rise to this challenge and we'll be able  to find the workers that are needed
 

